{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Creating a Gantt chart: Step 2"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when creating a Gantt chart."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations. Interpret the requirements report to determine the technological needs in managing information, based on rules and protocols established by the company."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through grammar and vocabulary structures.  Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "merge"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to join, to link"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In step number two we are going to identify task relationships and merge them."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tandem"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "one after the other"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are always tandem activities we need to complete."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "detached"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not linked to something else"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The detached activities are called parallel."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "assignment"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "task"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are also assignments we can perform at the same time."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "rely on"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to depend on"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We always write the number or letter of the assignment in which the sequential activities rely on."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bond"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "connection"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We write the type, and the bonds it has with the others."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Infinitives of Purpose and Simple Present Tense with Frequency Adverbs"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use the simple present tense with frequency adverbs to express how often a subject performs an action. In addition, you can add an infinite of purpose in order to specify the intention you have when performing the action. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>There are two kinds of <strong>frequency adverbs</strong>: <strong>definite</strong> and <strong>indefinite</strong> frequency adverbs. Definite adverbs express a specific moment while the others do not say exactly the moment the subject performs the action.</p> <p><strong>Indefinite</strong><strong>frequency</strong><strong>adverbs</strong> are usually placed between the subject and the verb.</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>always</strong> design a Gantt chart for my projects.</i></p> <p><i>He <strong>never</strong> finishes the tasks on time.</i></p> <p><strong>Definite</strong><strong>adverbs</strong> usually go at the end of the statement.</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>We check our schedule <strong>every day</strong>.</i></p> <p>You can also recognize definite and indefinite frequency adverbs because the definite ones are usually longer than the indefinite ones.</p> <p>With regard to the infinitive of purpose, add <strong>to +</strong><strong> verb </strong><strong>or</strong><strong>in order to + verb </strong><strong>so that you </strong>make the intention of your action clear.</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>Before starting our projects, we always design a Gantt chart </i><strong><i>to</i></strong><strong><i>associate</i></strong><i> the tasks</i><i> and ensure its completion. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</i></p> <p><i>Before starting our projects, we always design a Gantt chart <strong><i>in </i></strong><strong>order</strong><strong><i>to</i></strong><strong> associate</strong> the tasks and ensure its completion.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In this step, we are also going to identify task relationships and merge them in order to set our Gantt chart."}, {"type": "example", "content": "In order to consolidate our sequential tasks, we are going to write down next to each activity the letter S or P to differentiate sequential from parallel."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We sometimes use some acronyms to express the kind of relationships among these activities."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Explore more about the second step to create a Gantt chart."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Invent a dialogue with about 120 words using vocabulary taken from the lesson."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read your dialogue aloud and check your pronunciation with your instructor."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find more information about the topic."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a dialogue taken vocabulary from the lesson."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I read my dialogue and check my pronunciation with my instructor."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Types of Charts: Charts to Show Trends"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Charts are a great way to explain information in a simple and organized way. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>There are certain types of charts to show tendencies found in your data. For example, bar charts and line graphs show these trends.&nbsp;</p> <p>You can use certain charts to express trends; however, the best way to show patterns is using the line charts. These charts are useful when representing data that continually changes with time. In order to show data in a line chart, use a group of markers, or data points, and link them using straight line segments. Look at the following image of how a line chart looks:</p>"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob09img01.jpg"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Let's identify this vocabulary to use the acronyms in our chart."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le01ob09re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p>Welcome to the second part of how to devise a Gantt chart. We already consolidated our tasks in step one. In step two, we always have to correlate them in order to organize our schedule; this is going to help us to define the time each task is going to take. In this step, we are also going to identify task relationships and merge them in order to set our Gantt chart. First, you need to remember that there are always tandem activities that we have to complete, and there are also assignments we can perform at the same time.</p>"}, {"time": "38000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p>The ones that follow one another are called sequential because we can never start one without finishing the other since they depend on each other. On the other hand, the detached activities are called parallel. This means that we can sometimes work on two or more assignments at the same time without affecting any process. In order to consolidate our sequential tasks, we are going to write down next to each activity the letter S or P to differentiate sequential from parallel. We always write the number or letter of the assignment in which the sequential activities rely on, so we usually have two columns next to each task where we write the type and the bonds it has with the others. We sometimes use some acronyms to express the kind of relationships among these activities.</p>"}, {"time": "94000", "image": "enpe8n3le01ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p>Let's identify this vocabulary to use the acronyms in our chart. We use FS (finish to start) in front of a task to state that another task has to be totally completed&nbsp;to begin with this one. SS (start to start) means that an assignment cannot start until another activity does. FF (finish to finish) means a task cannot finish until another one does. Finally, use SF to express that a task cannot finish until another one starts; however, this is not very common in use.</p>\n<p>Remember always to use these acronyms to define the kind of relations among your activities.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le01ob09re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Le